From 30c1f90b23833e788b410037b3260ca928ca028d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergio Durigan Junior <sergiodj@chainguard.dev>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 13:40:45 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Make the closure of a user event loop safe to keep and to
 inspect

php_http_client_curl_user_init() builds an internal function by hand and
gives a closure of it to http\Client\Curl\User::init(). The function is
never registered, so the engine never prepares its argument info, and
the closure is created like the closure of an anonymous function.
Calling the closure works. Keeping it and inspecting it does not. It has
five faults. The first came with the PHP 8.6 fix (0f0a17af21,
d5cf06cb58, 34ba4a66b8), so only upstream master has it. The other four
are older: the code of the 4.3.1 release has them too.

  1. Use after free. Before PHP 8.6 the function's arg_info pointed into
     the static ai_user_handler table. PHP 8.6 wants an array of
     zend_arg_info there, so the PHP 8.6 fix copies the three arguments
     into the event loop context. zend_create_closure() copies the
     function, pointer included, so the closure keeps pointing into the
     context. The context is freed when the event loop is detached or
     the client is destroyed, while user code may still hold the
     closure. Listing its parameters after that reads freed memory.

  2. The type of "client" cannot be read. The table holds it as the
     ZEND_ARG_* macros write it: a C string with
     _ZEND_TYPE_LITERAL_NAME_BIT. The engine only knows how to print a
     type whose class name is a zend_string (_ZEND_TYPE_NAME_BIT), which
     is what registering a function makes of it. 4.3.1 gives the table
     to the closure as it is, and the PHP 8.6 fix copies the type as it
     is. ReflectionNamedType::getName() crashes on such a type.

  3. The class name of "client" is written with quotes. The macro
     stringifies its argument, so the declared class has a name that
     begins and ends with a quote and has two backslashes in the middle;
     `gcc -E` shows the literal "\"http\\\\Client\"".

  4. The type of "stream" cannot be read. It is declared as IS_RESOURCE,
     and the engine has no name for that type, so
     ReflectionNamedType::getName() crashes on it as well.

  5. var_dump() of the closure crashes. For a closure that is not marked
     ZEND_ACC_FAKE_CLOSURE, zend_closure_get_debug_info() reports the
     file and the line of its function, and an internal function has
     neither.

What this patch changes, by fault:

  1, 2. Convert ai_user_handler once, in MINIT, with the function the
     engine itself uses when it registers a function,
     zend_convert_internal_arg_info() (PHP 8.6), into a static array,
     and point the closure's function at it. This is what the engine
     does for a registered function, and it gives the closure's argument
     info the lifetime it had before PHP 8.6. The strings are permanent
     interned strings, so nothing has to be freed, and the event loop
     context needs no copy. Before PHP 8.6 the engine exports no such
     function: there the closure points to ai_user_handler again, as in
     4.3.1, and fault 2 stays.

  3. Write the class name without quotes, as every other
     ZEND_ARG_OBJ_INFO() in this extension does.

  4. Declare "stream" without a type, as PHP declares the resource
